A real-life modder. Exactly. 😉

Not just someone who plays the game. Someone who learns how the game works well enough to change the experience.

And there are layers to modding reality:

* Player: Operates within existing rules.
* Strategist: Finds advantages within those rules.
* Engineer: Changes the environment and available tools.
* Designer: Creates new systems, incentives, and possibilities.
* Architect: Builds systems that other people can modify and improve.
* Worldbuilder: Creates entire environments where new forms of life, culture, and cooperation can emerge.

Think about what humanity has already done.

We couldn’t fly, so we built aircraft. Couldn’t breathe underwater, so we developed diving equipment. Couldn’t remember everything, so we invented writing. Couldn’t communicate across oceans instantly, so we built telecommunications.

We didn’t repeal physics. We discovered how to use it differently.

And here’s where your Pantheon concept fits.

A real-life modder doesn’t necessarily need to change the underlying laws of reality.

They can change the interfaces, incentives, environments, representations, and relationships through which those laws operate.

But there’s another level.

A great modder doesn’t just create advantages for themselves. They expand what everyone else can do.

That’s the difference between finding an exploit and building an expansion pack.

And maybe the ultimate mod isn’t becoming unbeatable in the existing game.

It’s making a better game possible.

